<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ursum</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 BCE – 1640 BCE (approx.) · capital Ursum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ursum, a north Syrian city on the caravan routes to Anatolia, remembered for its long siege by the Hittite king Hattusili I.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urshu">Urshu</a> lay west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, probably near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaziantep">Gaziantep</a>, on the routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. It appears in Old Assyrian trade letters and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> archives as a city with its own king and a place where merchants traded.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its government. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> letters show it linked to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yamhad">Yamhad</a> in the politics of the 18th century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 17th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> besieged Ursum for months. A Hittite literary text, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-urshu">Siege of Urshu</a>, mocks his generals' failures. The city fell and lost its independence.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1900 BCE – 1640 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:21.89%;width:5.17%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 BCE – 1640 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century-bce">20th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ursum</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Urshu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1770&amp;focus=ursum" aria-label="Ursum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5507 760 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ursum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5935 1127h0M5852 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5827 1016h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5827 1016h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=-1770&amp;focus=ursum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

